  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose to use a genetic algorithm combined with local search within a Bayesian optimization framework. Unlike the previous approaches that relied solely on local search or population-based methods, we will dynamically alternate between exploration and exploitation phases. The core idea is to start with a population of candidate solutions, then iteratively refine them using local search, while maintaining diversity through crossover and mutation. The Bayesian optimization component will adapt the search strategy based on the estimated improvement potential, transitioning from broad exploration to intensive local exploitation as promising regions are found.\n\nThe key innovation is a new evaluation method that explicitly handles the boundaries between active and inactive regions, ensuring that the optimization respects the physical constraints and naturally weights the search toward minimizing overlapping integrals in a physically meaningful way.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: h(x) is represented as a piecewise constant function defined by 47 samples over [0,2] with dx=2/47.\n- Variables: h is a vector of 47 real numbers.\n- Constraints: \n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  - \u2211h[i] * dx = 1 (equivalent to \u2211h = n_points/2)\n- Objective: Minimize C5(h) = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx = max_k np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\")*dx\n\nThe optimization will use two key components:\n1. Repair algorithm to maintain constraints\n2. Local search using SLSQP with Bayesian adaptive stepsize\n3. Global exploration using DE crossover with variance-aware mutation\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe main function will:\n1. Initialize population with parents and random restarts\n2. Set up Bayesian parameter estimates for stepsize adaptation\n3. Iterate between exploration and exploitation phases:\n   - Exploration: Population-based search with DE crossover\n   - Exploitation: Localized search around best candidate using SLSQP\n4. Record best solution and exit when budget exceeded or convergence criteria met\n\nThe objective function includes:\n- Repair mechanism to enforce boundary conditions and normalization\n- Boundary handling to automatically manage the piecewise nature\n- Dynamic weighting to prioritize target regions\n\nHelper functions will:\n- Repair individuals: Scale to maintain sum and clip to [0,1]\n- Local refinement: Use SLSQP with custom gradient approximation\n- Diversity maintenance: Variance-aware crossover\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Population size: 150\n- Exploitation budget: 65% of overall budget\n- Exploration budget: 35% of overall budget\n- Adaptive crossover: 90% for early generations, 70% for later generations\n- Step-size control: Bayesian estimated improvement dynamically adjusts mutation\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Evaluation time per candidate: ~0.00017s\n- Maximum evaluations: 7.65e6 (1300 seconds budget)\n- Iteration breakdown:\n  - 50% used for exploration\n  - 50% used for exploitation\n- Failure recovery:\n  - If repair fails, restore best candidate from previous iteration\n  - If valid candidate, repair and rescore before continuing\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Validate candidate satisfies: 0<=h<=1 and sum==23.5\n- Compute C5 using other_candidates_evaluation(h)\n- Only return candidates with C5 reduction > 1e-8 from parent\n- Sanity checks included:\n  1. Check mass conservation\n  2. Verify domain that correlation is finite\n  3. Validate that overlap integral doesn't exceed smoothing threshold\n\nThe returned tuple satisfies:\n- h_values: array of 47 floats with sum=23.5\n- c5_bound: computed as the max overlap integral\n- n_points: 47\n\nImplementation assumes availability of:\n- np.correlate\n- scipy.optimize.minimize\n- Bayesian optimization via scipy.stats.gaussian_kde",
